const budgetTips = [
  { title: 'Kolkata is India\'s best value city', text: 'Kolkata has the lowest cost of living of any Indian metro. Dorm beds from ₹400, thali meals from ₹80, and metro rides for ₹5. A full day including entry fees, meals, and transport can cost under ₹1,000 ($12). The quality of food — especially street food and sweets — is among the best in the country at these prices.' },
  { title: 'Eat mishti every day', text: 'Bengali sweets (mishti) are a cultural institution. Balaram Mullick & Radharaman Mullick in Bhowanipur and KC Das on Esplanade are the heritage sweet shops. Mishti doi in a clay pot: ₹40–60. Rosogolla: ₹20/piece. Sandesh: ₹30–50/piece. These are meals, not snacks — the quality is extraordinary.' },
  { title: 'Take the tram at least once', text: 'Kolkata\'s tram network charges ₹7–10 per ride — the cheapest way to see the city slowly and historically. Route 6 (Esplanade to Kalighat) is the most atmospheric. The trams are ageing beautifully and are unlikely to survive much longer — ride them while they exist.' },
  { title: 'Kathi rolls are the definitive street food', text: 'Kolkata invented the kathi roll — flatbread wrapped around egg, chicken, or mutton kebab filling (₹60–120). Nizam\'s on New Market Row invented the format in 1932. Hot Kati Roll on Park Street and Campari in Sreebhumi are the current institutions. Avoid paying more than ₹150 for a roll anywhere.' },
];

